    "cade": {
      "word": "CADE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "Bred by hand; domesticated; petted. He brought his cade lamb with him. Sheldon.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "To bring up or nourish by hand, or with tenderness; to coddle; to tame. [Obs.] Johnson.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "A barrel or cask, as of fish. \"A cade of herrings.\" Shak. A cade of herrings is 500, of sprats 1,000. Jacob, Law Dict.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A species of juniper (Juniperus Oxycedrus) of Mediterranean countries. Oil of cade, a thick, black, tarry liquid, obtained by destructive distillation of the inner wood of the cade. It is used as a local application in skin diseases.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
      "relatedWords": []
    },
    "cadence": {
      "word": "CADENCE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "The act or state of declining or sinking. [Obs.] Now was the sun in western cadence low. Milton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A fall of the voice in reading or speaking, especially at the end of a sentence.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A rhythmical modulation of the voice or of any sound; as, music of bells in cadence sweet. Blustering winds, which all night long Had roused the sea, now with hoarse cadence lull Seafaring men o'erwatched. Milton. The accents . . . were in passion's tenderest cadence. Sir W. Scott.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Rhythmical flow of language, in prose or verse. Golden cadence of poesy. Shak. If in any composition much attention was paid to the flow of the rhythm, it was said (at least in the 14th and 15th centuries) to be \"prosed in faire cadence.\" Dr. Guest.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Her.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "See Cadency.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Man.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Harmony and proportion in motions, as of a well-managed horse.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Mil.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A uniform time and place in marching.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Mus.) (a) The close or fall of a strain; the point of rest, commonly reached by the immediate succession of the tonic to the dominant chord. (b) A cadenza, or closing embellishment; a pause before the end of a strain, which the performer may fill with a flight of fancy. Imperfect cadence. (Mus.) See under Imperfect.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To regulate by musical measure. These parting numbers, cadenced by my grief. Philips.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
